00:15:53.600 --> 00:16:00.240
URL just goes out into the world, and anyone who&nbsp;
has access to that can use it. But you don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:05.840
have a way of monitoring who has made copies&nbsp;
of your project and started their assignment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.040 --> 00:16:13.920
If a student wants help from you in a way where&nbsp;
they would want you to peek into their project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:19.040
you need to be granted permission in order to&nbsp;
be able to do that. So that's an additional step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.040 --> 00:16:24.160
for the students to have to do. Now there are two&nbsp;
other bullet points I want to discuss. And these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:29.920
could be pluses for some of you and minuses for&nbsp;
others. These permanent projects-- once a student&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.920 --> 00:16:34.800
makes a permanent project-- count towards&nbsp;
the student's free hours on RStudio Cloud.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.520 --> 00:16:42.800
for some this might be okay. Because maybe you&nbsp;
are teaching in a situation where you are not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.440 --> 00:16:48.880
going to be kind of responsible for the cost of&nbsp;
the compute your learners are going to be. So in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:54.320
that case this might be okay. Chances are if you're&nbsp;
teaching in a university setting, for example, where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:59.600
students, you know, have already paid for the&nbsp;
course and do not expect to incur additional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.600 --> 00:17:05.120
charges, they probably would not want to be put&nbsp;
in a situation where they have a limited number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.120 --> 00:17:10.560
of free hours to work on their assignment. We want&nbsp;
students to be work-- working on things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:16.720
as long as they want to and not have these kind of&nbsp;
artificial limits on their learning. Additionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:21.600
students control permissions. Is this a good&nbsp;
thing or a bad thing? iI again depends. If you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:27.520
teaching in a graded course you may not want this&nbsp;
because it's a too easy a way perhaps for them to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:34.160
share their work with their classmates, which&nbsp;
might constitute some sort of academic misconduct.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:39.200
But you might be teaching in a little&nbsp;
workshop where this is perfectly fine. So again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:45.600
depending on your situation, these could be&nbsp;
pros or cons. Let's talk also about inviting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.600 --> 00:17:51.520
to an RStudio Cloud workspace, which is the second&nbsp;
method of sharing your materials that I mentioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.520 --> 00:17:58.000
which is for longer engagement projects. So&nbsp;
here what we're going to do on the instructor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:03.600
view is you can see this is my account. I have&nbsp;
a bunch of projects to myself in my workspace.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:09.120
But I'm going to click on the little kind of&nbsp;
hamburger menu there, open the sidebar ,and create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.120 --> 00:18:14.400
a new space. Let's say I'm teaching a longer&nbsp;
workshop called Shiny Essentials. This was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:20.400
two day workshop I taught this year, so I figured&nbsp;
I'd reuse that name. And here I can see there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.400 --> 00:18:27.680
nothing in that workspace yet. I can create a new&nbsp;
RStudio project. And I can name it something.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:33.040
Maybe let's just name it 01 Getting started.&nbsp;
Again say that's going to be the first module&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:40.800
in this particular longer workshop that I'm giving.&nbsp;
So here again we have an RStudio Cloud project. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:40.800 --> 00:18:46.000
I can change the access level to everyone. But this&nbsp;
time it's not everyone in the world, it's everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.000 --> 00:18:51.680
in this workspace. And I'm going to turn it into&nbsp;
an assignment. So now when the students get invited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.680 --> 00:18:57.040
to my workspace and land here they're going to see&nbsp;
that they have some assignments they need to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:57.040 --> 00:19:01.600
working on. This is not to say it's like homework.&nbsp;
It's just something they can get started with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:08.320
And in fact on the student view they see the word&nbsp;
start instead of the word assignment there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:13.360
You can see from a different-- so&nbsp;
a workshop that i actually did give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.360 --> 00:19:18.480
that was designed in this way, for each&nbsp;
of the assignments that I have created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.480 --> 00:19:25.040
I can see the derived project. So I could actually&nbsp;
click on there and peek into students projects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:26.240 --> 00:19:31.280
And this is a good way-- the summary statistics&nbsp;
here are a good way of gauging for you how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.280 --> 00:19:36.800
students have started. But also you can deep down--&nbsp;
dive deeper and look into their projects. Which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:41.520
is really nice, especially if they need help and&nbsp;
you're teaching remotely and can't just kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:50.400
peek over their shoulder. In the Members tab of a&nbsp;
workspace that you create, like this, you have some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.400 --> 00:19:56.880
options. You have some options about how you want&nbsp;
to invite students to your workspace. I generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:03.280
use the sharing link. So it creates a single link.&nbsp;
And I can pick a role for people who come into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:03.280 --> 00:20:08.640
workspace using that link. And we'll say more about&nbsp;
the roles in a second. And simply either email that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:14.320
out. Or if iIm teaching, you know, share that during&nbsp;
the first few minutes of the teaching engagement.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:21.120
I've covered some names up here too, in order not to&nbsp;
show the real students names on the webinar. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:28.320
you can basically see that that would be how you&nbsp;
can keep track of who is in your workspace. So the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:34.320
four roles that are available are admin, moderator,&nbsp;
contributor, and viewer. And they have different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.320 --> 00:20:39.840
permission levels. So admin is probably you. And&nbsp;
you can do everything, including managing users.

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:47.840
A moderator is usually the level of access I&nbsp;
give my teaching assistants. They can view and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:52.720
edit and manage all projects. So&nbsp;
they can go in and help students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.720 --> 00:20:58.000
but they don't end up managing the users, the&nbsp;
actual students who get admitted into the place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.000 --> 00:21:04.720
Contributors are basically students who&nbsp;
can only see their work. And then viewers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:10.560
I generally use that for like an auditor in a&nbsp;
course. Or sometimes a guest, like a colleague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:16.000
will say, "I'd like to see how you're doing things."&nbsp;
But I don't necessarily want them to see the list&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.000 --> 00:21:22.800
of students, you know, in my course in the-- in&nbsp;
the members tab. So I give them that lowest level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:30.240
of access. We can also set permissions. These&nbsp;
boxes will be unchecked by default. And I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:35.600
encourage you to be carefully thinking about&nbsp;
checking them. So do you want your contributors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.600 --> 00:21:39.920
to see the members list? You know if you're&nbsp;
teaching a public course maybe not. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:39.920 --> 00:21:44.560
if it's a small university course and you want&nbsp;
everyone to know each other's names, maybe you do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.680 --> 00:21:50.640
Whether or not contributors, so that's students, can&nbsp;
make their projects visible to all members, again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.640 --> 00:21:57.680
we discuss this notion of potential academic&nbsp;
misconduct that could happen by students being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.680 --> 00:22:03.520
able to too easily share their work. Perhaps, you&nbsp;
know, not checking that box is not going to protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:08.720
you from, you know, everything that could go wrong.&nbsp;
But we don't want to potentially be additionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:14.560
making things easy for students if the way the&nbsp;
course is set up is such that they shouldn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:19.680
see each other's work. Or if it is that they should&nbsp;
be able to, then we could allow them to change that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.680 --> 00:22:25.360
And the third one is about whether they can change&nbsp;
project resources. And that's getting into things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:31.040
like the memory and the compute that they're going&nbsp;
to use, which gets tied into the pricing, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:31.040 --> 00:22:36.160
we'll say a couple things about at the very end.&nbsp;
So think about these permission levels. But it's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.160 --> 00:22:41.920
good to know that these are there, that you-- and you&nbsp;
can toggle based on your own teaching situation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:48.080
We also mentioned this notion of a base project.&nbsp;
So here in the workspace I've clicked on the gear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.080 --> 00:22:53.360
icon for the workspace itself, where I could&nbsp;
rename my course if i wanted to. But underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.360 --> 00:23:00.000
it you can see that I can set a base RStudio&nbsp;
project. So these base projects include-- they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:05.840
basically an any old RStudio Cloud project&nbsp;
that you kind of delegate as the base project.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:07.200 --> 00:23:11.600
This is where you can define the R&nbsp;
version that will be used for all projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:11.600 --> 00:23:17.600
what packages will be installed, and if there's&nbsp;
any certain files that you want repeated across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:17.600 --> 00:23:22.640
all of the projects in the course. For me this&nbsp;
is usually not data because i usually tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.640 --> 00:23:27.360
change, you know, data files from assignment to&nbsp;
assignment. But it's usually things like a code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:27.360 --> 00:23:32.560
of conduct, maybe submission instructions, things&nbsp;
that will stay constant throughout the assignment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:37.840
And you can update it as many times as you like,&nbsp;
which is really great. Because if you, you know, if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.840 --> 00:23:43.040
there's a new package comes out or a package gets&nbsp;
updated and you want to change things up in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:43.040 --> 00:23:47.920
middle of the semester, you can go back and do&nbsp;
that. And your changes are not retroactive, for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:47.920 --> 00:23:53.360
a good reason. You don't want to mess up students&nbsp;
work that happened prior to that time point. So any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:58.880
changes you make will only affect projects&nbsp;
that are created after that change was made.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:00.160 --> 00:24:06.240
So inviting in our-- inviting your students to&nbsp;
an RStudio Cloud workspace, lots of pluses in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.240 --> 00:24:12.320
my opinion, various permission levels, this notion&nbsp;
of base projects with desired packages installed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:12.320 --> 00:24:17.920
the notion of assignments, which remove the need&nbsp;
to remind students to make a copy of the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:25.200
before starting work, and this ability to peek into&nbsp;
student projects. Maybe on the minus side one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:25.200 --> 00:24:30.160
I can think of is that when students land in the&nbsp;
workspace they don't actually land in the RStudio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:36.480
IDE, right? They need to do an additional thing to&nbsp;
figure out which project to go into next. So they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:41.840
may need instructions, like start the assignment&nbsp;
titled getting started, or something like that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:48.160
Easy enough to handle, but something you need to&nbsp;
remember as the instructor. And again on could be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.160 --> 00:24:54.560
pro, could be a con side, student's work now counts&nbsp;
towards your, you know, this could be you as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:54.560 --> 00:24:59.360
instructor or your institution that might be&nbsp;
paying for the particular level of RStudio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:59.360 --> 00:25:05.040
Cloud for the workspace hours. And that could be&nbsp;
a positive or it could be a negative, depending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:05.040 --> 00:25:10.640
on your situation. An instructor controls the&nbsp;
permissions, and again depending on the teaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:16.560
engagement type you have that may be a positive or&nbsp;
a negative. I tend to find that for my University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:16.560 --> 00:25:22.320
teaching these are both pros for me. But for&nbsp;
other types of teaching engagements that might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:30.160
different. Let's wrap things up with a few tips for&nbsp;
teaching with RStudio Cloud. So one, the first is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:30.160 --> 00:25:36.080
that when you-- changes to an assignment a student&nbsp;
has started won't propagate to the student copy. So if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:40.800
you've set up an assignment for your students&nbsp;
and then realize there was a typo, or you forgot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:40.800 --> 00:25:46.880
to add a data file, you're not going to be able to&nbsp;
just change things up and then let it propagate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:46.880 --> 00:25:52.400
Once the students start their assignment they're&nbsp;
actually making a copy of that assignment, like at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:52.400 --> 00:25:57.280
that snapshot of time point. So you're going&nbsp;
to need to get that information to them in some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:02.800
other way. If you're teaching with git you could&nbsp;
be updating the GitHub repositories and they could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:08.080
simply pull the changes from there. Otherwise maybe&nbsp;
you reach out to your students in some other way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:13.360
to let them know of the corrections. Packages&nbsp;
in the base project are installed, but they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:13.360 --> 00:26:18.080
not loaded. So don't forget those library commands in the assignments that you give to the students.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:19.120 --> 00:26:24.640
The latest information is always on the RStudio&nbsp;
Cloud guide and the What's New page. And you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:30.400
get to these from the hamburger menu on&nbsp;
the left side of your RStudio Cloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.400 --> 00:26:35.760
window. So if you're thinking, "how do I do x?" the&nbsp;
guide is probably the first place to look at.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:41.600
And if you encounter any sort of slowness or&nbsp;
glitches, you know projects not starting up as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:46.720
quickly as they used to, the first place to&nbsp;
check is the system status. So you can take--&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:53.520
again you can from the left hand menu you&nbsp;
can get under help to the current system status.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:59.520
And if there is an outage that will be noted there.&nbsp;
If there isn't but you're still experiencing this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:59.520 --> 00:27:04.160
or your students are experiencing it,&nbsp;
then, you know, reach out to support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:11.680
to let them know. If you-- I would strongly&nbsp;
recommend creating an additional account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:11.680 --> 00:27:16.640
and logging in as a student to see what they see.&nbsp;
Because what you see is not always what they see.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.640 --> 00:27:21.040
And it's nice to be able to assure yourself that&nbsp;
you've set things up the right way. Especially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:26.400
the first few times you're teaching with a new&nbsp;
technology. Test assignments to set sufficient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.400 --> 00:27:33.600
computational resources. These are toggles that you&nbsp;
can change. So you can see those little toggles on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:33.600 --> 00:27:39.120
the side here. So make sure that you test to&nbsp;
make sure that you know the data size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:44.080
you have, or the computation you're asking your&nbsp;
students will actually work with the allocated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:49.440
kind of RAM and the CPUs. And if not you&nbsp;
will be able to toggle those up if you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:50.240 --> 00:27:58.320
The whole peeking at your students project&nbsp;
feature is fantastic but you, I believe you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.320 --> 00:28:03.120
might want to use that sparingly. Or at least&nbsp;
I try to use that sparingly. Because the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:03.120 --> 00:28:08.080
thing that I want to get my students to do is&nbsp;
to learn to articulate the issues they're having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:08.080 --> 00:28:13.840
without me going in and fixing it for them. That&nbsp;
being said, sometimes they may not have the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:19.760
vocabulary if they're for-- especially if they're,&nbsp;
you know, very new to computing, to R and RStudio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:19.760 --> 00:28:25.600
So it's nice to be able to you know push them&nbsp;
to articulate things. But if that's not getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.600 --> 00:28:29.920
anywhere and people are getting frustrated, or the&nbsp;
students is getting frustrated it's really nice to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:35.440
be able to peek in. And it's additionally helpful&nbsp;
for remote teaching situations, as you can imagine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:36.240 --> 00:28:41.680
The assignment feature, even though the word&nbsp;
is assignment, I try not to think of it as just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:47.360
homework. I also try to think of it as, you know, if&nbsp;
I'm going to be doing some live coding in class&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.360 --> 00:28:52.080
iId like to write out a skeleton for&nbsp;
myself where I will be filling in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:52.080 --> 00:28:57.360
code. And I use that feature to share&nbsp;
that code starter with the students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:57.360 --> 00:29:02.720
so that some of them, if they actually want to code&nbsp;
along with me, they have that to get started with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:11.680
A couple other useful resources, there is&nbsp;
now a nice playlist with 14 very short&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:11.680 --> 00:29:17.280
and informative videos about how to do much of&nbsp;
what i talked about and more with RStudio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.280 --> 00:29:24.800
Cloud. And there's a short link for it on the&nbsp;
top of the screen: rstd.io/cloud-playlist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:24.800 --> 00:29:30.480
So you can take a look at those and watch through&nbsp;
them. And if you are going to be using RStudio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:30.480 --> 00:29:35.600
Cloud for teaching or for other purposes where you&nbsp;
think there will be heavy usage, lots of students,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:43.040
or maybe lots of projects take a look at the&nbsp;
pricing options. There is always a free option.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:48.400
Although as soon as you're thinking about a&nbsp;
classroom with a substantial number of students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:48.400 --> 00:29:53.040
the free option may not be the right one for&nbsp;
you-- may not be sufficient anymore. So I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:53.040 --> 00:29:59.120
recommend especially taking a look at the Cloud&nbsp;
Instructor pricing plan, and also reaching out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.120 --> 00:30:05.040
to the RStudio sales folks who work on this to&nbsp;
just describe your situation of number of students&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:05.040 --> 00:30:10.240
and the type of course you're teaching to let them&nbsp;
help you help guide you to figure out the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:17.040
pricing option for you. Thank you very much for&nbsp;
tuning in, and I will hang out a little bit in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:17.040 --> 00:30:23.360
chat on YouTube as well to answer your questions.&nbsp;
Once again you can find the slides at rstd.io/tl-rscloud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:32.480
if you'd like to see a PDF&nbsp;
version of those as well. Have a great day!

